--TEST--
Core\Random::bytes and ::token each name which of their two checks refused an oversized draw, and token's bound sits at exactly half of bytes's — spec § 11, `rule:programs/memory-priority`, ADR 0063 R4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A draw too large is refused twice over, by checks that answer different
// questions: `nvs_runtime::affordable` is the policy seam `rule:programs/memory-priority`'s
// `[limits.hard]` ceiling will attach to, and the allocator is what refuses a
// buffer this machine cannot serve today. Which sentence comes back is what
// says which of the two stopped the call, so every row here reads the message
// rather than only that something threw.
//
// `Which` exists because a case's own helper has to be a method (ADR 0011 § 1)
// and every row below asks the same question of a different count.
final class Which {
    public static function bytes(uint $count): string {
        try {
            Core\Random::bytes($count);
            return "served";
        } catch (Throwable $drawn) {
            return $drawn->message;
        }
    }

    public static function token(uint $count): string {
        try {
            Core\Random::token($count);
            return "served";
        } catch (Throwable $rendered) {
            return $rendered->message;
        }
    }

    // Which check answered, with the member's own name off the front, so the
    // two members' answers can be compared to each other.
    public static function check(string $answer): string {
        if ($answer == "served") {
            return "served";
        }
        if (Core\Str::endsWith($answer, "the requested allocation is larger than any this process could hold")) {
            return "seam";
        }
        if (Core\Str::endsWith($answer, "the requested draw is larger than any buffer this process could hold")) {
            return "allocator";
        }
        return "some other refusal";
    }
}

// The bound between the two checks, named on both sides. The seam refuses only
// what cannot be allocated at all — a size past `isize::MAX` — so the last
// count it lets through is `isize::MAX` itself, and there the answer comes from
// the allocator instead. One count apart, and the sentences are different.
echo Which::bytes(9223372036854775807), "\n";
echo Which::bytes(9223372036854775808), "\n";

// `token`'s seam is asked about the *answer*, which is twice as long as the
// entropy drawn, so the same bound sits at exactly half. The count below it is
// the allocator's refusal — which is a throw rather than a process abort only
// because the draw is reserved fallibly, as `bytes`'s is.
echo Which::token(4611686018427387903), "\n";
echo Which::token(4611686018427387904), "\n";

// Counted rather than read off a line: a member that dropped the doubling, or
// asked the seam about its draw instead of about its answer, prints plausibly
// on all four rows above and fails here.
array<uint> $counts = [1, 1000, 1000000000000000, 4611686018427387903, 4611686018427387904, 9223372036854775807];
uint $two = 2;
int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($counts as uint $count) {
    if (Which::check(Which::token($count)) == Which::check(Which::bytes($count * $two))) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}
echo "token answers as bytes does at twice the count: ", $agreed, " of ", Core\Arr::count($counts), "\n";

// The halving is quantitative as well as a bound: not one of these counts is
// past `bytes`'s seam, and exactly the two whose double is past it are past
// `token`'s. What the other four meet is the allocator, or nothing at all.
int $seamForBytes = 0;
int $seamForToken = 0;
foreach ($counts as uint $each) {
    if (Which::check(Which::bytes($each)) == "seam") {
        $seamForBytes = $seamForBytes + 1;
    }
    if (Which::check(Which::token($each)) == "seam") {
        $seamForToken = $seamForToken + 1;
    }
}
echo "past the seam: bytes ", $seamForBytes, ", token ", $seamForToken, "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Random::bytes(): the requested draw is larger than any buffer this process could hold
Core\Random::bytes(): the requested allocation is larger than any this process could hold
Core\Random::token(): the requested draw is larger than any buffer this process could hold
Core\Random::token(): the requested allocation is larger than any this process could hold
token answers as bytes does at twice the count: 6 of 6
past the seam: bytes 0, token 2
